  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that the chemical's concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(t) & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3}+25 & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =125 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the chemical's concentration is changing when $t=5$ minutes is equal to ${C}^{\u2032}(5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+25) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{C}^{\u2032}(5)=3(5)^{2}=75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the chemical's concentration when its concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is  $75$ millimoles per liter per minute.",
